Date: Sun, 13 Mar 94 19:53:11 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-163] LANG: evolution of language

Nan Wolfslayer's reply:
>>Nan Wolfslayer writes:
>>>all image and imageless, like the ark of God.   [excerpt from Rilke]
>>>
>>Sounds like Jung's archetypes.  
>
>-- I thought of Jung when I was writing this post.  Does he believe that
>humans have a kind of pre-existent 'hardware' like Chomsky's idea on the
>basis of language as something 'built in'

Yes, the collective unconscious could be considered a pre-existent
'hardware' -- in fact I'd prefer to call it firmware and have the brain
and body as hardware. The personal unconscious would be read/write memory,
eventually getting filled with software.  Here's a little section on Jung
I downloaded, describing divisions in the unconscious psyche:

-----------------------excerpt from on-line Grolier's entry
                       (under Jung: Theory of Symbols)

                   ...To account for the many striking similarities
between independently originating symbols in individuals and across
cultures, he suggested the existence of two layers of the
unconscious psyche:  the personal and the collective.  The personal
unconscious comprises mental contents acquired during the
individual's life that have been forgotten or repressed, whereas the
collective unconscious is an inherited structure common to all
humankind and composed of the archetypes--innate predispositions to
experience and to symbolize universal human situations in
distinctively human ways.  There are archetypes corresponding to
situations such as having parents, finding a mate, having children, 
and confronting death, and highly elaborated derivatives of these
archetypes populate all the great mythological and religious systems
of the world.
-----------------------

In the nature / nurture debate, the notions that we either have no innate
characteristics or that everything comes pre-built, are both rather
indefensible positions.  But there's plenty of room for discussion over
the 'hardware/firmware/software' divisions.  

Take something like a popular catch-phrase, catchy tune, or clothes
fashion.  These all have to be represented by some type of structure in
the brain, we don't know exactly how, but the relevant structure gets
copied from one brain to another a great many times.  Such units of
cultural transmission have been described as "memes", a new type of
replicator.  Memes require brains to propagate in, just like genes require
lifeforms.  But as soon as any new replicators arise and conditions are
suitable for their propagation, a new form of evolution has been effected.

Now clothes fashions, for example, would be relatively short-lived memes.
These memes clearly exist in the personal unconscious or read/write
memory rather than the read-only inherited firmware of the collective
unconscious.  But it gets more interesting when you consider those memes
which have achieved long-lasting success in replication.  The idea of a
"god" has long filled a psychological need in a great many people; hence
the "god" meme has a very high survival value in the meme pool.  Other
memes, like those for earth, air, fire, water, death, and other concepts
which are rather universal in human experience, also find it especially
easy to fill their own niche; hence they are also particularly successful
in the meme pool.

But wait -- these memes that have been very successful for generations
correspond to Jung's archetypes!  It seems as if some mechanism might
exist whereby 'winning' memes are passed on to descendants, so the
most popular memes get written into the firmware, mankind's inherited
memory, the collective unconscious.  Certainly it would be much easier
to see how instincts or a "race memory" may develop.  

Still, I can't see how life experiences of individuals could get encoded
into DNA to be transmitted to offspring, and Lamarckian theories of
biological evolution are generally not accepted today.  It would be
rather neat if a suitable mechanism could be found.

>Anyway,
>my question was and is - how can something exsist in any form prior to
>creation.  Okay, okay, I'm not talking about genes or DNA, or my Mommy's
>egg, etc. because what I consider to be _me_ and what I consider essential
>to 'nanness' seems to be something more than all of my parts put together. 

Precisely, our unique individualities cannot be specified simply by DNA,
which is why we have to rely on cryonics and/or nanotech or uploading 
and/or life extension if we plan to survive.  And the notion that a whole
can only have the property of its parts would be a fallacy of composition.
In order to specify the individual, you would need the contents of the
personal unconscious -- memories of all life experiences (probably by
now inaccessible but not erased) acquired so far.  This cannot exist prior
to development, since the DNA blueprint could only specify the hardware,
firmware (collective unconscious) and a little more firmware (inherited
family traits).  Or it could only exist if you allow that the future exists
'now', in another part of spacetime.   

>> [description of the Ark of the Covenant's whereabouts elided]
>
>---I knew about this from watching Indiana Jones' movies, however, what is
>inside this Ark?  Is it God's promise?  To whom?  What for?  If it's got
>this great gold-chest then I bet it is something really neat.  

The Ark was supposed to contain two stone tablets on which the ten
commandments were written, Aaron's rod, a pot of manna, and was a focus
of the divine presence.

>>In common with us, it's an evolving spontaneous order. 
> 
>---Do we evolve within language or does language evolve within us?

The easiest example of language evolving would be the emergence of
neologisms and abandonment of disliked / redundant words.  The structure
representing each particular word, its meme, is replicated as it
propagates from brain to brain -- a subdivision of memetic evolution.
Those words with the higher survival value catch on, and the unpopular
ones go out of circulation.  However, vocabulary would be stored in
read/write memory.  If other aspects such as grammar and phoneme usage
are stored in a nation's collective unconscious / read-only-memory, it
seems less sensible to think of this as 'evolving' independently of us.

>>States and ecosystems could never be conscious in this way -- they could
>>never experience meaningful subjective mental events.
>
>-- I'm not sure I agree with this.  Do you have to be subjective or 'other'
>(i.e., aware of your otherness) to be conscious.  I'm not sure that you do.

Some say that thermostats are "conscious" or have mental states, and
ecosystems have a greater variety of states.  But if mental states are
different states of being, the thing needs to have some way of representing
concepts and percepts so that it knows what different states of being are.
A thermostat might know which state it was in, hot or cold, but it wouldn't
know what hot and cold meant.  To be conscious as I'd understand it, it
would need about 12 properties that I suggested in a post back in July.

> In my culture (Eastern Cherokee) we are taught that ecosystems ARE alive
>in a way that is different that human awarness, yet still conscious.  As
>for a state, if you had several distinct personalities which were
>unconscious of each other but exsited as different 'states of being' within
>one body - would each individual state be conscious of an awareness of
>their identity?  I'm going to have to think about this some more.

I don't believe 'ladder of selves' theories of identity are a suitable  
analogue to states, since the individuals comprising a state are not
wired together -- it would take telepathy to produce a group mind.

