Steve Whitrow wrote 3/2/94: <That would describe the "man-made" s.o.'s such as
money and memetic evolution, but aren't there also natural s.o.'s such as
biological evolution and self-regulation of ecosystems?>

It is not proper to make such a distinction, all human activities are natural.

Steve again 3/5/94: <But supposing we have a goal-based or ancient liberties
interpretation of natural rights:  (i)  When humans are free to pursue their own
chosen goals, they are led by "an invisible hand", a spontaneous order, to
promote unintended ends, which are frequently in the best interests of human
society.

I like this formulation much better than your (ii) and (iii) formulations.  I
don't like the "objective rights" idea deriving from "inherent nature of man."
The "inherent nature of man" is subjective free will, an unrestricted space of
potential purposes, none of which have any "objective rights" clinging to them a
priori.  Subjective purposes are not "discovered," they are chosen.  Rights
exist only in consensual reality ("We") not in objective reality.  Their origin
is in subjective purpose.

Steve again 3/12/94: <Universality (all rational beings) seems to leave scope
for individual interpretation -- do fetuses / babies / children / backward
people count
as rational, and could future AIs ever be classed as rational?>

Let me blather on a bit to establish some background.  In evolution,
consciousness has gone from simple "knower-and-known" recognition systems of
single-cell life, up through the reptile brain, to the primary consciousness of
lower mammals to the higher consciousness of humans (following Edelman's
terminology).  Sentience is primary consciousness, unable to construct a social
consensus based on knowledge of the past projected into the future.  Sapience is
the ability to make the future fit whatever individual purpose an entity has.
Increases in consciousness have been modular additions to neurophysiology,
humans only need to decide what modules to add on next for an increase to the
next level of consciousness.

Universality is not related to rationality, but to the capacity of an individual
entity to choose a subjective purpose.  A choice to be rational is one possible
purpose.  A choice to be moral is another.  A key portion of the test of
sapience (human, AIs or whatever) and the source of universality: the ability of
an entity to assign itself purposes first-person subjectively, unknowable to any
other entity outside of itself, and unchangeable by any other entity outside
itself; these internally chosen purposes, may or may not be communicated to
others at the choice of the entity and can override any built-in values.

There is a hierarchy of the spontaneous order of consciousness which is why
there is discussion of animal rights.  As we move to higher levels of
consciousness (post-human), discussion of human rights will be exactly analogous
to discussion of animal rights today.  Fetuses/babies/children/backward
people/Artificialis Sapiens have this potential for subjective free will.  Until
the potential manifests itself into actuality, some custodial arrangement is
necessary, that is, limited rights.

Steve again: <Although if someone claims that rights are just a convenient
fiction which we use to help achieve our goals, to say that goals are a
convenient fiction which help to enforce our rights doesn't sound quite right.>

Let me cover the four possible combinations of rights and enforcement using
examples from the U.S. Constitution:
(1)  Rights are enumerated and enforced.
(2)  Rights are unenumerated and enforced.
(3)  Rights are enumerated and unenforced.
(4)  Rights are unenumerated and unenforced.

(1)  Enumerated rights, such as the Bill of Rights, are a formalization of
consensual purpose, a historical process.  Universality only applies to the
extent of the consensual purpose and no further.  Uniform enforcement by
legitimate authority deriving from the consent of the governed, is the mark of
the ordered-by-design society, such as the project of the Enlightenment.

(2)  When rights are not enumerated, as in Amendment IX, then consensual purpose
is enforced through the invisible hand.  My favorite combination.  This is the
spontaneously ordered society.

(3)  When enumerated rights are unenforced it means that the consensual purpose
underlying the formalization is no longer present.  An example of unenforced
rights are the individual rights defined by Amendmant XIV, the equal protection
clause.  When the judicial branch, executive branch and legislative branch all
conspired to introduce group rights (affirmative action; minority contract
set-asides; de facto quotas in business and government arising from the
Disabilities Act, Civil Rights Act, etc.; the inability of organizations to hire
or fire without being sued capriciously for discrimination as a homosexual, a
disabled person, an inability to speak English, an ethnic group, a minority, a
woman, sexual harassment, etc.), individual rights became unenforced by a shift
in consensual purpose.  The reason consensual purpose established individual
rights to begin with, is that this is what is required for the largest possible
coherent society.  The reason we now have group rights is because when "We the
People" are split up into groups, there are many consensual purposes of smaller
size, aka factions, exactly what the Founding Fathers feared most and the reason
for the construction of limited government with checks and balances.  The checks
and balances failed when group rights were introduced because statist power can
be increased by the legitimate reason that only a powerful state can coerce
factions (smaller conflicting consensual purposes) into a functioning state
without devolving into violence and lawlessness.  Thus, it was the consensual
purpose of the governing elite to factionalize the country in order to increase
their own power to shape their destiny through enforcement of their consensual
purpose.  This is the totalitarian temptation and it will be part of us as long
as we have an independent ability to shape the future to our purpose.  America's
slide into group rights and repudiation of individual rights has placed it on
the road to totalitarianism, justified by the need to establish order between
squabbling factions which the governing elite brought about itself in order to
have this justification.  This is the society ordered-by-coercion.

(4)  This combination is where consensual purpose is either too diffuse or is
changing too quickly.  It is chaotic, like a transition state within society,
possibly revolutionary times or a quirky, capricious dictatorship.  It generally
doesn't last very long before one of the other three combinations returns.

Steve again: <We might be able to pick some axioms and find that increased
prosperity is an almost certain conclusion, but what about socialists and
statists?  They aren't going to be convinced by a lack of empirical evidence
(they normally aren't convinced by evidence either).>

The technological/economic take-off models detailed in Kauffman's "Origins of
Order" book show exactly this, an almost certain conclusion.  The only thing
really required is a critical mass of consensual purpose.  The socialists and
statists have a consensual purpose that will conflict to the end with any
attempt to radically shift large numbers of people away from it.  It's not going
to be easy, it requires a vigorous, powerful philosophical thrust, propagated
widely.  At present, it seems the entropic philosophies hold a commanding lead
and are gaining steadily because the education system and the
media/entertainment complex are disabling rationality and creativity wholesale.

A topic never far from my thoughts is the potential for restricting our
self-determination and losing control of our destiny (i.e., dependency or
slavery).  My fear is that without some fairly robust consensual purpose
world-wide, regarding individual rights to establish private purposes without
external domination, that dehumanization tendencies will increase our chances of
extinction.  The technological advances in biophysics will give us enormous
production capacities with any and all life existing now or created by us in the
future.  It is easy to see where all life will become simply a product, subject
to property rights or state sovereignty, as so many animals and plants are now
to us.  It is not difficult to see how all life could become viewed as merely
products traded on the commodity markets with totalitarian regulators.

