rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray) writes:
>
>  I'm not really well-read on philosophy, but didn't Hume argue that
>the only real knowledge is logical/mathematical (i.e. the relationship
>among ideas), that we can have no knowledge of matters of fact (i.e.
>about nature, existence), and therefore science is a fraud, blah blah.
>It is also my impression that Hume is accepted by most modern philosophers.

Yes, but the story doesn't end with Hume.

I think most philosophers would agree that Hume was right as far as he
goes, but that doesn't mean that everyone should give up trying to learn
anything.  Hume was reacting to Descartes, who tried to derive everything
he believed from pure logical deduction, including the existence of God,
and the reliability of sensory perception, among other goofy things.  Hume
pointed out that if you try to do this, you can't take any sense data into
account, because it could always be erroneous.  This severly cuts down on
what you can believe.

>...
>  Of course, the only thing this tells me is that philosophy is pretty
>irrelevent when it comes to how people treat the real world, i.e. researchers,
>engineers, and theorists really don't care whether or not someone philosopher
>has shown that there is no logical basis for empiricism/causuality because
>the assumption that there is a basis leads to more useful things than not
>assuming the basis.

Well, I suppose philosophers could just sit in their ivory tower, looking
down at the scientists in their slightly shorter ivory tower, and say to
each other "Look at those dopey scientists!  They think it's possible to
know things by perceiving the world!  Why don't they listen to Hume, and
just give up?"  But that's not much fun, or very enlightening, so ever
since Hume, epistemologists have been coming up with reasons to belive
things other than pure logical deduction.  Pancritical rationalism is the
latest version of a system for deciding what to believe.

Beth Preston said "Kant said that the great scandal of philosophy was that
no one had succeeded in proving the existence of an external world;
Heidegger replies that the real scandal is that anyone should ever have
thought it necessary to try."

