Date: Mon, 14 Mar 1994 00:35:49 -0100
Subject: [#94-3-171] Art of Extropian interest

This posting is a series of brief reviews, and responses to reviews, of
comics, books and movies from an extropian point of view.  I will only
spoil the endings of things I don't like; otherwise I will preserve
suspense.

ON DEADLY GROUND (movie)

Hoo boy, is this bad!  I liked Steven Segal's previous movie, Under Siege,
even though I was rooting for the bad guys, on account of how they were
anarchists who wanted to set up a free market in nuclear weapons.  But this
movie is environmentalist and antirationalist propaganda of the worst kind.
 Plus, even if I agreed with the message, it's just a stupid movie-- the
script is very sloppy, and I never felt much suspense.  The crowning
goofiness is that at the end of the movie, after our "hero" has killed
dozens of law-abiding people and destroyed billions of dollars worth of
property, he is invited to the state capitol building to give a speech to
the adoring masses, in which he tells them that (a) oil companies have
suppressed the invention of a car that runs on water, and (b) it's time
"the people" got together and made sure "big business" is never allowed to
produce anything that might harm anyone anywhere in any quantity
whatsoever.  Hopefully when "the people" watch this movie, the absurdity of
(a) will make them question the superficial political desirability of (b).

IN THE NAME OF THE FATHER (movie)

Watch this movie and get your anarchist juices flowing by watching the
British government screw over perfectly innocent people.  All the actors
turn in magnificent performances, and it's a very affecting story.  The
director has a nice sense of color, too.

ARISTOI by Walter Jon Williams (book)

I'm reacting to the review by Mark Plus in a recent ExI newsletter.  He
didn't like the book because the protagonist has anti-extropian
motivations.  But I think he missed the point by identifying the worth of
the book with the motivation of the protagonist.  At the end, extropian
memes triumph despite the best efforts of the protagonist.  I thought it
was a terrific book, and well worth reading.

BATTLE ANGEL ALITA by Yukito Kishiro (comic book)

A tale of romance and adventure among cyborg transhumans struggling to
survive in the nooks and crannies of a posthuman world.  It's got gorgeous
art, cool violence and a touching teen romance with surgery.  

I've only read part two, which is seven issues long.  There are also parts
one and three, which I haven't been able to scrounge all the issues of, so
I don't know if they're as good, but I don't see why not.


