Subject: [#94-3-172] LANG: Evolution of language

Steve Whitrow writes:

> The great thing about biological evolution is that you can start with 
> primitive, simple objects, and end up with advanced, complex, conscious
> lifeforms.  It seems as if the changes are directed towards an end state

But this sort of teleolgical view is not accepted in modern biology, for
a very good reason:  If the changes "are directed", when who is the Director? 
Furtermore, the idea that evolution entails an increase in complexity has 
fallen out of favor in biology, probably for similar reasons.  Perhaps you are 
thinking in terms of the Anthropic Cosmological Principle?


> -- production of the complex lifeforms.  Now if evolution has to be
> advancement towards some end state, then viruses haven't evolved since
> Pre-Cambrian times, and insects stopped evolving in the Carboniferous
> period.  (Although it might not always be possible to be entirely
> objective about whether a change is an advancement, a stagnation, or a
> decline.)  If we accept this definition, language could still be
> considered to be evolving (albeit very slowly), but only if you expect it
> to be more advanced in the future than it now is.  

How would you measure "advancement"?  Even if you could make such a
measurement, why would you expect language to be more "advanced" in
the future than it is now?  Also, there is no "end state" in evolution, 
just a tendency to evolve to fit a particular niche.  When the niche changes
or goes away, so will the species; if the niche remains stable (as in 
the case of sharks), so will the species.

Language doesn't evolve; it just changes.  If the human species evolves
(or self-modfies) biologically, then our language may very well be
different -- perhaps even more complex (though I don't think anyone knows
how to measure linguistic complexity either).  

-- Simon!

