Short answer first:  solid reference = Physics Reports vol 190, no 6,
pgs 309-403.  The entire issue is a review article on inflation, as it
stood in 1988(?) or thereabouts (I forget the date) by KA Olive.  On the
specific subject of eternal inflation/ quasi-steady state see "From the
big bang theory to the theory of a stationary Universe" by Andrei &
Dmitri Linde and Arthur somebodyorother in _Physical Review D_ 49(4) pg
1783.  Unfortunately all the authors know far to much about the subject
to be accessible to the non-specialist  :-)

I fear my own exposition would be too idiosyncratic, so, for a run-
through try this, from New Scientist 26 Feb 94 "Inside Science" series,
"Birth of the Universe" feature, Page 2, Box 1.

Note that the "big bang" is now being differentiated from the "moment
of creation".  As light is cast on these subjects, previously the domain
of cranks and mystics (like the Pope), terms that were being used
synonymously are now resolving into distinct labels.

Notation:  3 x 10^5 = 300,000
           5 x 10^-4 = 0.0005
*********** start of Box 1 ************

1: What happened before the big bang?  Inflation

According to a popular but very speculative theory, first proposed by
the American physicist Alan Guth, when the Universe was about 3 x 10^-44
seconds it began to expand exponentially fast.  This so-called
_inflationary epoch_ ended at about 3 x 10^-35 seconds.  Afterwards, the
Universe continued to expand but at a rate that was enormously slower. 
It is this slower expansion which we have come to call the big bang.

So violent was inflation that some have likened the big bang to a hand
grenade going off in the middle of a nuclear explosion.  During
inflation, space blew up, or "inflated", until it was 10^50 time bigger
than it had been.  The entire observable Universe, with all its stars
and galaxies, would in this picture have inflated from a region of space
smaller than an atom.  Incredibly, the energy that drove this enormous
expansion came from the vacuum itself - empty space.

Inflation is a bizarre theory, which is almost impossible to understand
unless you are particle physicist!  It is an outgrowth of our modern
view of the vacuum.  Far from being empty, the vacuum is now known to
be seething with "quantum fluctuations", subatomic particles and their
antiparticles which the _Heisenberg uncertainty principle_ allows to
appear out of nothing and annihilate each other in the blink of an eye.

The principle arises because the act of measuring the energy of a
microscopic entity is intrusive, changing the energy in an unpredictable
way.  So, the energy of the vacuum fluctuates and this energy can create
particles because of E = mc^2.

The "virtual" particles of the vacuum have real and peculiar
consequences.  Not only do they create a small mass density even in
empty space but associated with this is a "negative pressure", a sort
of tension in space.  In normal circumstances, this pressure is
minuscule but when the Universe was 3 x 10^-44 seconds old, and the
density of both matter and the vacuum was 10^94 grams per cubic
centimetre, a very special "vacuum-like state" was created in which the
negative pressure was overwhelming.  It manifested itself as a kind of
gravitational repulsion.  This provided the enormous "push" which
started the expansion of the Universe.

But the vacuum was unstable.  Like an atom in an excited state, it
wanted to return to the ground state.  After about 3 x 10^-35 seconds,
it decayed into the familiar vacuum we know today.  The consequences of
this were dramatic.  During its exponential expansion, the density and
pressure had remained constant - another bizarre property of the special
vacuum state.  This meant there was an enormous amount of energy
available.  When the vacuum decayed, the energy went into the creation
of ordinary particles moving near the speed of light.  The Universe
became hot, its temperature leaping to 10^27 K.  This was the hot big
bang.

Only a tiny "seed" of matter was needed in the Universe before
inflation.  The balance was created from the vacuum.  This had lead to
the suggestion that the seed was a quantum fluctuation, making, as Guth
put it, the Universe the "ultimate free lunch".

As for what happened before inflation, here we are into even more
speculative realms.  In some inflationary scenarios, the observable
Universe is no more than a bubble inflating in one corner of a greater
universe.  Elsewhere, forever inaccessible to us, may be an infinity of
other bubbles like the froth on a great sea.

The principal problem that inflation solves is the so-called horizon
problem.  This is that the temperature of the cosmic background
radiation is virtually the same in regions of the sky which could not
have been in contact at the epoch of the last scattering.  They were
beyond each other's "horizon".  Inflation solves this problem because
our entire Universe inflated from a minuscule regions of space,
different portions of which could easily have been in touch.

************* end of Box 1 **********

The principal prediction that inflation makes is that the Universe
should contain just enough matter so that the gravitational pull of the
matter _and_ vacuum together are enough to reduce the expansion towards
to zero but not quite enough to ever halt and reverse it.  Since the
amount of matter in the Universe seems to be very far short of the
required amount then either we will discover some "hidden" or "dark"
mass or the vacuum is contributing (via the Cosmological Constant) to
the energy density of space.  Either way direct and indirect
astronomical data from the Hubble Space Telescope and others are
expected to resolve this within the next few years.

